{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED on exact clean local head 250d0e33e71b4e7c738687f466f53566b8933469 and artifact 4eba55df. The three-part adoption, publication/reconciliation, and replacement split is a strong simplification, and acceptance now correctly uses a ratifiable proposal rather than ratify(artifact). Architecture: this is design-only and changes no current contract, but it describes future Workroom application governance and merge semantics plus layer 7 CLI/publication surfaces. The architecture page need not change in this head; the note must accurately state those boundaries before it can govern implementation. Security and authority blockers remain. First, the adoption table says an author or generic ratifier may ratify a review verdict; KindReport uses SatisfierOriginatingRequester, so only the review requester may do that. Second, the note never orders filing and ratifying the adoption proposal relative to merge, then tells implementation to rest on the post-merge artifact alone. That loses the proposal authority: assigned implementation rests on its request, whose chain must reach the ratified proposal and live artifact; self-initiated implementation rests on the motivating ratified proposal and should also cite the live artifact. State both paths explicitly. Third, deletion still performs a bare gs supersede from branch-controlled supersedes metadata before review and merge. That is the irreversible pre-merge authority defect the prior verdict forbade. All replacement retirement, including deletion, must be merge-sealed. Fourth, cross-author replacement is not defined: current merge may retire only paths directly covered by the signed artifact set, while the note neither requires review to co-sign N and S nor defines the signed replacement map and receipt validation that would authorize the old path. Specify one complete merge-sealed mechanism or defer the algorithm. Fifth, the reminder-hook design still uses ambient exec gs and does not preserve existing hooks, require shebang/executable mode, or safely handle shared core.hooksPath, and the publisher still omits the required bounded NUL-safe rules for refs, paths, renames/deletions, tags, force pushes, YAML, act count, partial recovery, and ineffective acts. Sixth, the evidence claim does not satisfy the citation condition: 1660ed25 and cdd4f708 are trailing suffixes that neither Git nor gs inspect resolves, and the third request-vs-promise artifact has no event id. Use canonical event IDs or an established resolvable display form for all three. The artifact also says 352 lines while the exact file has 357. Finally, the branch is not pushed: origin/notes/notes-and-decisions-draft remains 075fa9a3, contradicting the request and artifact claim that 250d0e33 is pushed. Security posture around post-push reconciliation, process/worktree identity, fail-closed attribution, and no irreversible pre-push publication is otherwise correct. Simplification: keep the three independent parts; make part one a complete authority chain, part two one explicit post-success publisher/outbox with a safely specified optional reminder, and part three one merge-sealed map with no branch-time supersession. make docs and git diff --check pass at the exact clean local head.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:4eba55dfb4a5ddf1ae56cec8e8693a412667c12d","head":"250d0e33e71b4e7c738687f466f53566b8933469","verdict":"changes-requested"}}